	affiliations = {The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong; Institute of Future Cities, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong},
	abstract = {Extreme heat events in densely populated tropical cities increasingly undermine the performance of conventional passive cooling strategies in low-income housing. Motivated by the urgent need to develop occupant-responsive housing solutions amid escalating extreme heat events in high density cities, the approach addresses the declining performance of traditional passive measures based on the indoor-outdoor temperature differential. This study presents a comprehensive design approach that synergizes conventional passive cooling strategies with observed adaptive occupant behaviour to enhance thermal resilience in low-income housing within tropical, high-density urban environments. Hong Kong serves as case study, where building simulations are conducted using prevailing climatic conditions. Three scenarios were developed. The first scenario incorporates internal and spatial modifications to establish localised microclimates. The second scenario evaluates the efficacy of envelope interventions to control solar heat gain. Scenario 3 combines spatial and envelope modifications. The core methodology is comprised of simulation tool EnergyPlus and inferential statistics. Quantitative analyses, including linear regression modelling and Sobol based global sensitivity analysis, assess the impact of these design interventions on cooling loads and thermal comfort. The results indicate that while conventional passive cooling measures struggle during extreme heat events, the combined adaptive spatial and passive façade strategies have synergistic effects, yielding substantial reductions in cooling load from 1634 to 443 kWh (73 % reduction) and significant improvements in comfort hours from 496 to 833 h (68 % increase). Overall, this integrated approach bridges passive technical performance with adaptive occupant-centric design and offers scalable recommendations for enhancing thermal comfort in low-income housing. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
	author_keywords = {Adaptive behaviour; High-density living; Low-income housing; Occupant-guided design; Subtropical climate},
	keywords = {China; Hong Kong; Architectural design; Behavioral research; Cooling; Economic analysis; Housing; Solar energy; Space heating; Thermal comfort; Tropics; Adaptive behaviour; Cooling load; Cooling strategy; High-density living; Low-income housing; Occupant-guided design; Passive cooling; Performance; Subtropical climates; Thermal; climate conditions; cooling; design; extreme event; human behavior; low income housing; Sensitivity analysis},
