	abstract = {Increasing energy needs in urban environments demand necessary improvements in building designs toward energy efficiency, sustainability, and comfort. This review explores some of the state-of-the-art technologies and strategic approaches for energy-efficient façade design in buildings, such as adaptive, double-skin, and photovoltaic-integrated façades. The paper assesses recent innovations of smart material technologies that enable dynamic response of the façade to environmental conditions and enhancement of thermal performance to contribute toward Net Zero Energy Building (NZEB) goals. Façade systems are reviewed according to their roles and impacts on reducing energy consumption, cutting carbon emissions, and improving indoor environmental quality (IEQ) significantly. Additionally, this review discusses the challenges and future opportunities for integrating these systems in different climates and urban settings. The comprehensive assessment offers valuable insights for architects, engineers, and policymakers working to create sustainable, resilient, and energy-efficient buildings. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd},
