	title = {Investigating tree shading and thermal variations in a subtropical zone: Single Tree to Urban Squares},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {Building and Environment},
	volume = {283},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.buildenv.2025.113408},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010842104?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Chair for Strategic Landscape Planning and Management, Technical University of Munich, Emil-Ramann-Str. 6, Freising, 85354, Germany; State Key Laboratory of Subtropical Building and Urban Science, Guangzhou, 510641, China; School of Architecture, South China University of Technology, Guangzhou, 510641, China; School of Architecture, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, New Territories, Hong Kong; School of Architecture, Southwest Jiaotong University, Chengdu, 611756, China; Department of Architecture, Tianjin University, Tianjin, 300072, China; School of Architecture and Urban Planning, Guangzhou University, Guangzhou, 510006, China; School of Civil Engineering, Chongqing University, Chongqing, 400045, China; School of Agriculture, Food and Ecosystem Sciences, The University of Melbourne, Burnley, 3121, VIC, Australia},
	abstract = {Urban trees are widely regarded as effective solutions for mitigating microclimate. However, fewer studies focused on the spatiotemporal characteristics of their thermal effects on adjacent spaces, particularly in urban squares where design and land constraints restrict extensive tree planting. In Guangzhou, China, this study first analyzed how a single tree influences energy components and microclimate variations in its adjacent space, considering dynamic shading effects. Subsequently, we used a validated ENVI-met model to assess physiological equivalent temperature (PET) variations across 36 planting scenarios that combined three tree species, four spacing to crown diameter ratios, and three line-planting patterns (One-line, Two-line, and Staggered). Results revealed that tree shading reduced PET by up to 4.7 °C, with surrounding areas experiencing an additional 1.1 °C reduction in the afternoon due to extended cooling effects. In shaded areas, sensible heat flux (Q<sub>h</sub>) was mainly driven by negative heat storage (Q<sub>s</sub>) from the underlying surface. Interestingly, tall trees with high canopies provided limited cooling directly beneath them but were more effective in adjacent spaces during morning and afternoon hours. Furthermore, cooling benefits exhibited threshold effects and delayed temporal responses. The Two-line configuration with a spacing to crown diameter ratio of 1:1 demonstrated the highest efficiency, balancing tree quantity and PET reduction. These findings improve understanding of urban trees’ thermal effects and offer design recommendations for the squares and similar open spaces to maximize cooling potential. © 2025},
