	abstract = {Energy efficiency in buildings is critical for decarbonization, especially in tropical climates where minimizing heat gain is essential. This study introduces a novel self-shading design that uses varied modular block geometries to enable adaptability in static self-shading facades. We demonstrate that the proposed design significantly reduces absorbed solar radiation, contributing to meaningful energy savings in building operations. In addition, with the purpose of accelerating assessment progress, we develop an efficient computational framework for evaluating self‑shading performance by comparing two Graph Convolution Network (GCN)‑based approaches. Our results indicate that an indirect multiscale learning framework—GCN + Neural Network (NN)—outperforms a direct GCN approach, achieving a lower Mean Relative Rrror (MRE) while using less memory and computational time. This scalable method offers a promising alternative to traditional Finite Element Method (FEM) simulations for assessing complex self‑shading designs. © 2025},
