	affiliations = {School of Urban Planning and Design, Peking University Shenzhen Graduate School, Nanshan District, Shenzhen, China; Department of the Built Environment, Eindhoven University of Technology, Eindhoven, Netherlands},
	abstract = {The built environment is pivotal to achieving global climate targets, yet electrification and renewable energy integration strain aging electricity grids. This study addresses these challenges through a decision-making support tool designed for energy self-sufficient greenfield developments. Combining hourly-resolution modeling of energy demand, renewable supply (solar, wind), and communal storage, the tool bridges spatial planning and energy transition goals. Evaluated via two Dutch case studies, the tool demonstrated how to optimize self-sufficiency by up to 100% through communal storage integration, dynamic shading, and wind energy adoption. Results revealed that while photovoltaic (PV) systems dominate supply, their seasonal variability necessitates oversized storage without complementary wind energy. The tool's iterative development incorporated expert feedback, refining datasets with 10-year averaged climate profiles and COP-driven efficiency adjustments. Practical insights include the need for multi-tiered dashboards to serve diverse stakeholders and predefined building typologies to enhance early-stage usability. Despite limitations in dwelling-type scalability and financial assessment, the tool proves critical for guiding sustainable urban design, emphasizing storage, and balancing grid resilience with decarbonization targets. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
	author_keywords = {Communal energy storage; Energy self-sufficiency; Energy transition in built environments; Hourly-resolution modeling; Renewable energy integration; Sustainable urban design},
